</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5.1: </span>Aggregate effects across agents and tracks. <math id="Ch5.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes BERTScore (mutated <math id="Ch5.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> original) computed against the <math id="Ch5.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-NN average.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Ch5.S1.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall results of all tests can be seen above: <math id="Ch5.S1.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <span id="Ch5.S1.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mutated <math id="Ch5.S1.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> original</span>; BERTScore is <span id="Ch5.S1.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1 to the <math id="Ch5.S1.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-NN average</span> (mean over the five most similar golden exemplars per item). One-sided tests reflect a preregistered expectation of improvement.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ch5.S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1.4 </span>Outcome of analysis</h4>

<div id="Ch5.S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="Ch5.S1.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Ch5.S1.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Where mutation helps most.</span> Comment-level guidance benefits substantially for both agents (<span id="Ch5.S1.I5.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">moderate <math id="Ch5.S1.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> large <math id="Ch5.S1.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></span>), with the <span id="Ch5.S1.I5.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">largest gains</span> for Neurodiversity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S1.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expert-suggestion alignment diverges by agent.</span> No measurable gain for <em id="Ch5.S1.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gendered Expert</em> suggestions; <em id="Ch5.S1.I5.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Neurodiversity Expert</em> shows a <span id="Ch5.S1.I5.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">small–to–moderate</span> but consistent improvement.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S1.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical implication.</span> For deployment, <span id="Ch5.S1.I5.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">prioritize integrating the mutated Comment prescriptions</span> into the Job Writer’s synthesis step; <span id="Ch5.S1.I5.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">revisit the Gendered Expert mutation objective/prompting</span>, as current settings do not shift its similarity distribution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S1.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why Expert cloning underperforms Comment cloning.</span> Golden <em id="Ch5.S1.I5.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Comment</em> references were <em id="Ch5.S1.I5.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">richer/longer and more actionable</em> than the concise <em id="Ch5.S1.I5.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Expert</em> cues, providing a <em id="Ch5.S1.I5.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stronger learning signal</em> to the mutation agent. This quality gap explains larger <math id="Ch5.S1.I5.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> values on Comments.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ch5.S1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1.5 </span>Scope of Experiment and limitations</h4>

<div id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scope.</span> Results pertain to two JobFair bias-mitigation agents (Gendered Language; Neurodiversity) acting over <span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">English-language</span> job descriptions from a curated corpus (300 per agent). Similarity is measured with <span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BERTScore-F1</span> using <span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">roberta-base</span> as the encoder. For each item, the score is computed against the <math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-NN average</span>—the mean BERTScore to the five most similar golden exemplars drawn <span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">from other jobs</span>—yielding a <span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cross-item, multi-reference</span> evaluation. The estimand is the within-item change <math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\mathrm{BERTScore}_{\text{mutated}}-\mathrm{BERTScore}_{\text{original}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>BERTScore</mi><mtext>mutated</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>BERTScore</mi><mtext>original</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\mathrm{BERTScore}_{\text{mutated}}-\mathrm{BERTScore}_{\text{original}}</annotation></semantics></math> per track (Expert, Comment).
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key limitations.</span></p>
<ul id="Ch5.S1.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ch5.S1.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric/encoder sensitivity.</span> Using <span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">roberta-base</span> trades precision for speed; absolute magnitudes may shift under larger or task-adapted encoders (e.g., <span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">roberta-large</span>, DeBERTa-v3-large). However, directionality of effects is expected to be stable.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S1.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ceiling and range compression.</span> Under cross-item, <math id="Ch5.S1.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-NN averaging</span>, BERTScore cannot reach 1.0 and the score range is compressed. Consequently, <span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">small absolute gains</span> (<math id="Ch5.S1.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx+0.007" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.007</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx+0.007</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="Ch5.S1.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.011" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.011</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.011</annotation></semantics></math>) can reflect <span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">meaningful behavioural drift</span>, but may also mask larger underlying qualitative improvements.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S1.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Golden-set heterogeneity.</span> Golden references vary in richness: <span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comments</span> are longer and more actionable than <span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expert</span> cues. This asymmetry creates a stronger signal for the Comment track and likely inflates the observable effect size relative to Expert.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S1.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalizability.</span> Findings are specific to the JobFair domain, agent prompts, and the curatorial criteria for golden exemplars. Transfer to other sectors, languages, or evaluation schemas is untested and may require re-tuning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S1.I6.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I6.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I6.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Statistical choices.</span> One-sided tests were pre-specified given an improvement hypothesis. With large <math id="Ch5.S1.I6.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, very small <math id="Ch5.S1.I6.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> can be statistically significant; we therefore report <span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cohen’s <math id="Ch5.S1.I6.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></span> to contextualize practical impact. We ran four primary comparisons (two agents <math id="Ch5.S1.I6.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> two tracks) without adjustment; given extremely small <math id="Ch5.S1.I6.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> values for three comparisons, corrections would not change conclusions, while the null result (Gendered Expert) is robust to any reasonable correction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S1.I6.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I6.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I6.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dependence structure.</span> Items may share topical overlap across job descriptions. Our paired design mitigates between-item confounds, but residual correlation could slightly narrow CIs; the Wilcoxon analysis helps guard against distributional assumptions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S1.I6.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S1.I6.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.I6.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.I6.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reproducibility details.</span> Exact library versions, tokenization settings, and retrieval parameters influence BERTScore at the third decimal place. We recommend archiving code, seeds, model checkpoints, and the golden index to ensure determinism.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our measurement choice BERTScore-F1 with roberta-base and <math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-NN averaging under cross-item matching provides a conservative, decision-useful lens on behavioral cloning. While stronger encoders or alternative metrics (e.g., BLEURT/COMET or task-specific critics) could sharpen estimates, the direction and relative ordering of effects are unlikely to invert.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evidence of behavioural cloning.</span> Across three of four primary comparisons, <math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> (mutated <math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> original) is consistently positive, statistically significant by both parametric and non-parametric tests, and accompanied by non-trivial effect sizes (moderate<math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>large for Comment; small<math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>moderate for Neurodiversity Expert). This pattern indicates that the silver dataset (system outputs) has shifted measurably toward the golden style, i.e., behaviour cloning has occurred under RAG-conditioned mutation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scale depends on golden-set quality.</span> The magnitude of cloning is modulated by the quality of the golden references. Tracks whose golden exemplars are longer, denser, more actionable, and stylistically consistent (here, Comments) yield larger <math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and tighter CIs, whereas tracks with shorter or sparser cues (here, Expert suggestions) exhibit attenuated gains or null effects (Gendered Expert). Under the <math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-NN averaging protocol, higher-quality and more homogeneous golden pools raise the effective upper bound for similarity and reduce variance, amplifying observable <math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, heterogeneous or low-signal golden pools dilute the target style and cap the attainable uplift.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implications.</span> (i) For product integration, the demonstrated comment-level gains satisfy deployment targets; encoder upgrades can be treated as optimizations. (ii) For dataset strategy, additional curation of the golden set especially the Expert track (longer, prescriptive, canonicalized phrasing, consistent taxonomy)—should increase the amplitude of cloning on Expert outputs. (iii) For evaluation, maintaining high-quality golden references is not merely cosmetic: it directly governs the measurable scale of behavioural transfer from golden <math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> silver outputs under the <math id="Ch5.S1.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-NN evaluation scheme.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ch5.S2" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5.2 </span>Results of Extraction Diagnostic (Neurodiversity Agent)</h3>

<figure id="Ch5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15366v1/figures/diagnostic_neuro.png" id="Ch5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/216;" width="381" height="216" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5.3: </span>Box plot of Extraction Diagnostic Parameters</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Ch5.S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For confidentiality purposes, we will limit this report only to 1 agent type for both ED and BD agents. We created a box plot for better visualization of results (<a href="#Ch5.F3" title="Figure 5.3 ‣ 5.2 Results of Extraction Diagnostic (Neurodiversity Agent) ‣ Chapter 5 Results" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> Figure). We evaluated the neurodiversity-focused agent and its extraction capabilities using a six-facet ED rubric with weights:
<em id="Ch5.S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Correctness</em> 0.35, <em id="Ch5.S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Completeness</em> 0.30, <em id="Ch5.S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Over-Extraction</em> 0.15, <em id="Ch5.S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Detail Accuracy</em> 0.10, <em id="Ch5.S2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Terminology Consistency</em> 0.05, <em id="Ch5.S2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Reasoning Alignment</em> 0.05.
Facet scores lie in <math id="Ch5.S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="Ch5.S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=13</annotation></semantics></math> documents as the number of documents in golden dataset. ED used all <math id="Ch5.S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=13</annotation></semantics></math> documents sampled to cover [job profiles main domains ] and length deciles. While underpowered for fine effects, bootstrap CIs are reported; findings are presented as indicative and inform subsequent larger runs.</p>
</div>
<section id="Ch5.S2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Descriptive statistics by facet (<math id="Ch5.S2.SSx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=13</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="Ch5.S2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Ch5.S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ch5.S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Completeness</span> — mean <span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.486</span> (95% CI [0.331, 0.641]); sd 0.244; median 0.55; IQR 0.338; min 0.00; max 0.78</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness</span> — mean <span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.479</span> (95% CI [0.308, 0.650]); sd 0.269; median 0.44; IQR 0.468; min 0.00; max 0.88</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Over-Extraction</span> — mean <span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.521</span> (95% CI [0.350, 0.692]); sd 0.269; median 0.56; IQR 0.468; min 0.12; max 1.00</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detail Accuracy</span> — mean <span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.614</span> (95% CI [0.456, 0.773]); sd 0.249; median 0.60; IQR 0.188; min 0.00; max 1.00</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning Alignment</span> — mean <span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.613</span> (95% CI [0.472, 0.753]); sd 0.222; median 0.60; IQR 0.113; min 0.00; max 0.90</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S2.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Terminology Consistency</span> — mean <span id="Ch5.S2.I1.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.679</span> (95% CI [0.527, 0.831]); sd 0.239; median 0.70; IQR 0.125; min 0.00; max 0.90</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ch5.S2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">What the metrics say about extraction quality</h4>

<div id="Ch5.S2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall pattern:</span> <span id="Ch5.S2.SSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">precision-leaning, recall-limited</span>. When the agent extracts, it typically chooses the right label and attaches the right attributes; however, it <em id="Ch5.S2.SSx2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">misses many gold items</em> and occasionally adds extras. Because the highest weights sit on <span id="Ch5.S2.SSx2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness</span> and <span id="Ch5.S2.SSx2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Completeness</span>, these weaknesses dominate the overall score.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch5.S2.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Ch5.S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ch5.S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Terminology Consistency (0.679)</span> — <span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">strong.</span> The agent usually uses the canonical labels expected by experts. <em id="Ch5.S2.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Typical failure:</em> picks a alternative category instead of the required subtype or a near-synonym outside the gold taxonomy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detail Accuracy (0.614)</span> — <span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">good.</span> When an entity is captured, attached attributes (e.g., severity, timing, laterality, test values) are often correct. <em id="Ch5.S2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Typical failure:</em> attribute “bleed” from nearby entities; off-by-one spans; temporal phrases misapplied.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning Alignment (0.613)</span> — <span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">good/medium.</span> Evidence trails the expert chain reasonably well. <em id="Ch5.S2.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Typical failure:</em> multi-sentence justifications lose a step; cites a plausible but not the decisive sentence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Over-Extraction (0.521)</span> — <span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">medium.</span> Extras exist but aren’t rampant. <em id="Ch5.S2.I2.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Typical failure:</em> duplicates via surface-form variation; hedged statements treated as positives.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S2.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Completeness (0.486)</span> — <span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">weak (recall).</span> Misses a substantial portion of expert findings. <em id="Ch5.S2.I2.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Typical failure:</em> low-salience mentions (lists/tables/discharge notes), implied findings, long-tail terms, and dispersed evidence (entity and attribute separated across sentences).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ch5.S2.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ch5.S2.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness (0.479)</span> — <span id="Ch5.S2.I2.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">weak (exact-match).</span> A sizeable fraction of extracted items do not exactly match the gold label/entity. <em id="Ch5.S2.I2.i6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Typical failure:</em> subtype vs parent confusion; merging adjacent entities; spans that include tokens altering meaning.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ch5.S2.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Aggregate performance</h4>

<div id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By manual check mean EDScore has an estimated <span id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">95% CI <math id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 5.11\pm 0.73" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>5.11</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.73</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 5.11\pm 0.73</annotation></semantics></math></span> (<math id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=12</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating <span id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mid-tier extraction fidelity</span> with substantial case-to-case variance driven mainly by dispersion in <span id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness</span> and <span id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Completeness</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our result:</span> <span id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5.11 / 10 <math id="Ch5.S2.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Low quality extraction relative to expert</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ch5.S2.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Human validation vs. Judge behaviour</h4>

<figure id="Ch5.T2" class="ltx_table">
